export const METHODOLOGY_VERSION = "methodology-1";
export const METHODOLOGY_TEXT = "# Methodology statement, version 1\n\nMethodology statement `methodology-1`, written 2026-09-29 for engine 1.30.0\nand later. It says how the water-balance app turns a catchment's inputs into\nthe results its reports and evidence packs print. It summarises\n`docs/model.md` (the full reference, by section number below) and does not\nreplace it. This file is never edited once published: a change is a new\nversion (`docs/methodology/README.md`).\n\n## 1. What the model is\n\nA **daily water balance** of one river catchment, modelled as a tree of\nnodes (farms, stand-alone dams, other water users, gauges) that drain to one\noutlet. Every day it works out:\n\n1. the catchment's **natural flow** from rain, with a published rainfall–runoff\n   model (§2);\n2. each node's share of that flow, and what reaches it from upstream (§3);\n3. what each node's water use takes from the river, its dam and transfers, and\n   what it returns (§4);\n4. the flow left in the river at every node and the outlet, compared with the\n   **Environmental Water Requirement (EWR)** at each EWR site (§5).\n\nThe engine is deterministic: the same inputs and engine version give the same\nresults, to the bit. It rounds nothing inside the model (model.md §2.1);\nreports round for display only. Flows are held in m³/day. Every run records\nits engine version and a snapshot of every input, with a SHA-256 of each\ninput series, so a stored run can be re-run and checked.\n\n## 2. Rain to natural flow\n\n- **Runoff model: GR4J** (Perrin, Michel & Andréassian 2003), a four-parameter\n  daily rainfall–runoff model, written from the paper's equations\n  (model.md §2.4a). The earlier recession routine of the b023 workbook was\n  removed in engine 1.0.0 because it did not conserve water at the event scale\n  (engine-audit.md H1).\n- **Rain used** is the catchment's own rain record. Days it lacks are filled,\n  in order, from rain-source periods (another gauge for part of the record,\n  §2.4e), CHIRPS satellite rain bias-corrected per calendar month against the\n  catchment record (§2.4b), and the forecast (§2.4f). Runs of zeros that look\n  like missing readings are treated as missing unless confirmed dry (§2.4c),\n  and multi-day accumulations are spread over the days they cover (§2.4d). An\n  areal rainfall correction may scale the gauge to the catchment (§2.4g).\n  Every filled or changed day is marked in the run.\n- **Evaporation**: A-pan evaporation (monthly, or a daily series, §2.3a),\n  converted to potential evaporation with a pan coefficient, or a monthly PE\n  row entered directly.\n- **Land cover** (invasive plants, plantations) reduces the runoff of the\n  nodes it covers (§2.5a).\n\n## 3. Sharing the flow among the nodes\n\nThe catchment's natural flow is split into each node's own runoff by area,\nby a high/low rainfall split, or by entered shares (model.md §2.5). Shares that\nadd up to more than the catchment refuse the run. Flow then passes down the\ntree in calculation order, upstream first; a gauge only passes flow on.\n\n## 4. Water use\n\n- **Irrigation demand** is crop area × A-pan evaporation × monthly crop factor,\n  less effective rain, per day (model.md §2.3). An application efficiency and a\n  loss-return fraction split what is abstracted into crop use, return flow and\n  losses (§2.7, audit N1).\n- **Dams** store runoff and upstream inflow as the farm's settings direct, lose\n  evaporation and seepage and gain rain on their surface (§2.7a), and follow\n  their release and operating rules. A dam can be surveyed (a stage–storage\n  curve) or estimated from its capacity.\n- **Transfers and river off-takes** move water between nodes by structured\n  rules (months, maximum rate, minimum dam level) the same day (§2.6, §2.6a).\n- **Other water users** (towns, industry) take by seniority and position\n  (§2.7c); **demand objects** add non-crop demand at a node (§2.7f).\n- **Groundwater**: boreholes and their stream depletion, off by default (§2.7d).\n- **Supply rules** decide the order a farm draws from its sources (§2.7e).\n- **Development over time**: a dam's capacity may change during the run\n  (sediment lost from its surveyed capacity, a dam that holds water only from\n  the day it enters service), and a unit's abstraction may start on a date,\n  so the record is run with the works as they were on each day (§2.7g).\n- **Registered volumes** may cap a unit's use, or scale its demand to the\n  registered volume in a full-allocation run (§2.12a).\n\n## 5. The river and the Reserve\n\n- **The pragmatic EWR** is a daily requirement at the outlet, fragmented to the\n  farms by their share of the natural flow (model.md §2.5). The days it is not\n  met, and the shortfall volume, are counted at the outlet and each site.\n- **Reserve compliance** at an EWR site with a rule table (a Desktop Reserve\n  Model or gazetted table: maintenance and drought low flows, freshets and\n  floods) is judged month by month by the Reserve's assurance rules: the\n  requirement is read at the natural flow's percentile, and the month is met\n  or not on volume and days (§2.9c, §2.9d).\n- **Who is charged for a shortfall**: each day's shortfall at a site is split\n  among the users upstream of it in proportion to their net impact; the part\n  natural flow could not have met is nobody's (§2.7b). Curtailment targets\n  are worked out from those charges over the reporting window (§2.11).\n- The model **reports** the EWR; it does not protect it. Water users take first\n  and the EWR gets what is left, as in the b023 workbook, so every failure is\n  visible rather than hidden by a rule.\n\n## 6. Calibration, checks and uncertainty\n\n- **Calibration** fits GR4J's four parameters to an observed flow record at the\n  outlet (gauge or logger), scoring the simulated outflow, with the farms and\n  dams in place (model.md §2.10, §2.10b). The objective (by default KGE′), the\n  window, the periods excluded with their reasons, and each day's quality flag\n  (suspect, infilled, above the gauged range; §2.10h) are recorded with the\n  fit. Split-sample validation is run with every automatic fit, and a dry →\n  wet test when the record has at least 4 water years of 180 or more observed\n  days.\n- **Statistics** reported: NSE, KGE′, NSE of log flows and percent bias, with\n  Moriasi et al. (2007) ratings and their caveat that the thresholds were set\n  for monthly flows (§2.10f).\n- **WR2012**: the natural mean annual runoff is compared with the scaled\n  WR2012 quaternary reference, and flagged when it departs (§2.10c).\n- **Data checks** on the inputs: agreement of the observed records, double-mass\n  breaks, CHIRPS against the catchment rain by water year, zero runs and\n  accumulations (§2.10a), and the hydrologist's plausibility checks (§2.10d).\n- **Self-checks**: every run re-checks its own mass balance, each node's\n  workings and the EWR charges, and records the result (the engine's\n  `verify` checks). An invariant suite and a random-catchment soak test the\n  engine itself.\n- **Uncertainty**: a behavioural ensemble of GR4J parameter sets (GLUE-style:\n  sets accepted by stated thresholds, with the seed drawn by the database and\n  every start kept), whose bands are the range across the accepted sets. A\n  change between two runs is the **paired** difference over the same sets\n  (§2.10e). A band is not a confidence interval.\n\n## 7. What the model does not cover\n\n- Dam safety (classification and approvals for a dam with a safety risk,\n  form DW793), groundwater beyond the borehole depletion above, water\n  quality, and the socio-economic factors of s27 of the National Water Act.\n- Whether any water use is lawful. The model runs the use it is given.\n- A licence decision, which belongs to the responsible authority.\n- Travel time in the river and canals: a day's water reaches the outlet the\n  same day.\n- The open items of the engine audit, printed with every run as the **known\n  limitations** (docs/engine-audit.md), and the known engine bugs of the run's\n  version, printed as **errata** (docs/engine-errata.md).\n\n## 8. Assumptions a user sets\n\nThese change results and are printed with every evidence report: the runoff\nmodel's parameters and their source (a stored fit or entered), the\ncalibration record and window, the EWR tables and sites, the flow-share\nmethod, the pan coefficient or PE row, the CHIRPS correction and rain\nhandling, the attribution rule, the reporting window, the assurance\nthresholds, the allocation mode, the development dates of dams and\nabstraction, and the operating rules of dams and transfers.\n";
